{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>בִכְנָפָיו</b> — together with its wings; he need not pluck its wing-feathers. \"כְּנָפָיו\" — actual feathers, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 1:17). But this is not correct, for if so, Scripture would not specify from which place he splits it — whether from its front or from its back. Rather, its meaning is: and he shall split it at the place of its wings, for the בֵּי\"ת is a בֵּי\"ת of containment, as you say בָּאָרוֹן בַּבַּיִת or בַּשָּׂדֶה. And \"כְּנָפָיו\" is not feathers, but rather it is like every occurrence of כְנָפַיִם in Scripture: \"כֹּל צִפּוֹר כָּל כָּנָף\" (Genesis 7:14), \"יִפְרֹשׂ כְּנָפָיו לְתֵימָן\" (Job 39:26), and likewise \"בִּכְנַף מְעִילוֹ\" (I Samuel 15:27). And נוֹצָה is the hair that is on the body of birds, as in the verse (Ezekiel 17:3): \"הַנֶּשֶׁר הַגָּדוֹל גְּדוֹל הַכְּנָפַיִם אֶרֶךְ הָאֵבֶר מָלֵא הַנּוֹצָה\", and likewise we learned (Teharot 1:3): the wings and the feathers are impure and impart impurity and join together, and they said (Torat Kohanim after chapter 12:2): this excludes the hartom and the tzippornayim and the wings and the feathers."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:52.028663691Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}